   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 3,
    "topic_code": "3.15",
    "topic_title": "Carrying Out a Chi-Square Test for Homogeneity or Independence",
    "stem": "A random sample of 150 students at a large high school was classified by grade group and preferred lunch option. Underclass students (90): hot meal 52, salad bar 6, packed lunch 32. Upperclass students (60): hot meal 23, salad bar 14, packed lunch 23. The expected counts are 45, 12, 33 for underclass and 30, 8, 22 for upperclass. The chi-square test for independence gives χ² ≈ 10.30 with df = 2 and p-value ≈ 0.006. At α = 0.05, which statement correctly completes the test?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The expected count of 8 for upperclass salad bar is less than 10, so the expected-counts condition fails and the chi-square test should not be carried out.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This applies the large-counts threshold of 10 from proportion tests to a chi-square test. The chi-square condition only requires every expected count to exceed 5, and 8 does, so the test is valid. Fix: Check each expected count (not observed count) against 5 whenever you run a chi-square test for homogeneity or independence."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "All expected counts are greater than 5, so the condition is met. Because 0.006 < 0.05, reject H₀: there is convincing evidence of an association between grade group and lunch preference among students at this school.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The smallest expected count is 8, and the chi-square condition requires every expected count to exceed 5. The p-value is below α, so we reject H₀. The conclusion is stated in context, refers to the school's population, and uses non-definitive \"convincing evidence\" language for the alternative hypothesis of association."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "All expected counts exceed 5. The p-value of 0.006 means there is only a 0.6% probability that grade group and lunch preference are independent, so reject H₀.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This misreads the p-value as the probability that H₀ is true. A p-value assumes H₀ is true. It gives the probability of getting a χ² value of 10.30 or larger by chance alone under that assumption. Fix: Phrase the p-value as \"assuming no association, the probability of χ² ≥ 10.30 is 0.006.\""
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "All expected counts exceed 5. Because 0.006 < 0.05, the test proves that being an upperclass student causes a student to prefer the salad bar.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses definitive language and makes a causal claim. A test only provides convincing evidence, never proof. The data come from a random sample, not an experiment, so the test can show an association but cannot show cause. Fix: Ask whether treatments were randomly assigned before claiming causation, and write \"convincing evidence of an association.\""
     }
    ]
   },
